<div class="meta-item"><span class="meta-label">Version</span><span class="meta-value">1.0</span></div>
</div>
<div class="audience-banner"><p><strong>Audience:</strong> Department Web Content Managers and the District Web Team. Led by Sean A. Russell. Certification deadline day, what directors will see, how a department without a website gets one, and a live walkthrough of the audit rules with your feedback on whether each one makes sense.</p></div>
<nav aria-label="On this page" class="page-index"><p class="pi-label">On this page</p><ol><li><a href="#g1">The Meeting</a><span class="pi-count">2 sections</span></li><li><a href="#g2">What We Decided</a><span class="pi-count">2 sections</span></li><li><a href="#g3">Your To-Do List</a><span class="pi-count">2 sections</span></li><li><a href="#g4">Resources and History</a><span class="pi-count">1 section</span></li></ol></nav>
<h2 class="lead-heading">Summary</h2>
<div class="section-block lead-summary">
<p class="gap-note">The recording will be added here once it is posted.</p>
<p><strong>Certification deadline.</strong> Thursday, November 5 was the extended deadline. At the start of the session, 53 of 77 department WCMs were registered. Sean is reaching out to anyone who has not finished, and to their directors, to see what support they need. Starting Monday, November 9, there are no more extensions: anyone who has not completed certification loses access until they do. If you finished, congratulations.</p>
<p><strong>Canvas and LAB credit.</strong> Ernesto Gonzalez asked whether he still needs the Canvas assignment. One certification step assumed you already had Finalsite access, so a few new WCMs got stuck on it. That step is fixed, and people should no longer be stopped there. Canvas was only a link to the course, so it cannot mark you complete in LAB. Sean will close out LAB credit by hand for everyone who is certified. If you have your certification badge, you are done and there is nothing more to do.</p>
<p><strong>A department with no website yet.</strong> Ernesto Gonzalez asked how his department gets a website. The first step is for his director to talk with the other directors, because whether departments within ISD have their own sites has been an open question for years. Once the director says yes, schedule time with Sean for a crash course on the page and best practices, build the site, bring it to Hot Lab for feedback, and Sean gives it a once-over before you publish. Where it sits on the ISD main page is worked out after that.</p>
<p><strong>What directors will see.</strong> Patricia Sapp asked whether directors can see what needs to be updated on their department's page. Sean showed the director page and how it is being reworked into one overview, so a director does not need to go anywhere else. At a glance: whether their WCM is certified or how close they are, the next quarterly meeting, any open actions, the department's analytics with a short explanation, Hot Lab notes, and the widgets other departments use. It will have a short guided tour.</p>
<p><strong>Your meeting after the audit.</strong> Once you complete your audit, you can schedule a meeting with Sean. The meeting is about marketing, not the audit: what is possible with the tools and what your department wants to do with its website. Sean will look through your site before the meeting and bring a short list of ideas. The WCM attends. Your director is encouraged to come too, since they can ask questions in real time. The page will include a checklist to prepare.</p>
<p><strong>Public School Impact Application.</strong> Sean showed the new form built for Facilities and Real Estate Planning. It replaces a manual process of emails and PDF forms: the applicant pays on the e-store, fills out the form, uploads the receipt, site plan and survey, signs, and submits. The department reviews and accepts it from the back end, the applicant is notified, a 30 business day review clock starts with a reminder five days before the deadline, and approval notifies the applicant and archives the record. If your department has a similar process, let Sean know.</p>
<p><strong>The audit rule review.</strong> The audit is a new tool, but the audit itself is the same process as last year. There are about 36 rules it can flag. The goal is to go through each rule's wording together so that if one shows up on your list, you know exactly how to fix it. If you still do not know, the item links to the certification module that covers it, and Still stuck? sends your question to the District Web Team. Questions that many people ask tell us what needs a video or more support. Items only Finalsite can fix are being removed from your lists, so your counts may go down. For each rule, raise your hand if it makes sense as written.</p>
<p><strong>Rule: no text-heavy images or flyers.</strong> Screen readers cannot read text inside an image. If an image carries important information, that information must also be on the page as text. Alt text is rarely long enough for a flyer. Do not post a screenshot of a whole PDF: use an image from it and lay the information out on the page. The District is moving away from PDFs. Flyers make sense in emails, the Happenings and print, but on the website, the page is where the information lives. Ideally each campaign has its own graphic for the website, for email and for print, and the District hopes to support that with templates. The audit cannot read the banner, so this one is a spot check: if the image follows the rule, or has no text at all, confirm it and move on. Patricia Sapp and Ernesto Gonzalez said it makes sense as written.</p>
<p><strong>Rule: linked PDFs are tagged for accessibility.</strong> This is the issue Patricia Sapp raised earlier. A tool is being built to check any PDF, even a phone photo of a page, and fix it with one button if it is not accessible. It will also be available as a widget and inside the audit. This rule's wording will be redone once the tool is ready.</p>
<p><strong>Rule: headings are in order.</strong> Headings should not jump levels, such as H2 straight to H4, and every page needs a true H1, so the outline makes sense to screen reader users. Jennifer Amador said it makes sense to people who attend Hot Lab, but may not for those who do not. Sean showed an example on Finalsite: a Directory page with an H1 followed by an H3, which should be an H2. A common reason for skipping H2 is that it looks too big, especially with a long title. Sean will propose smaller heading sizes, show them side by side, get feedback from the District Web Team and from you, and check whether the sizes can be changed site-wide so no page needs to be touched.</p>
<p><strong>Rule: the overview page is labeled with the department name.</strong> On a large District site, a menu item called just Overview or Home does not say where you are. Sean showed how to add an overview page using Academics as the example: create the page, put it under the department branch, save, and publish. Without it, someone on a sub-page has no link back to the department's main page in the navigation. This is a Finalsite quirk. Most of you already have one, since it was a big item in last year's audit. If you are building a new site, it is one of the first things to do.</p>
<p><strong>Still stuck? status.</strong> Patricia Sapp said her Still stuck? question shows "waiting for an answer from District Web Team" on her side. Sean confirmed questions are coming in, and the District Web Team is making sure they are easy to find and answer.</p>
<p>The rule walkthrough continues at the next Hot Lab.</p>
</div>
